ind of  sugar fast food or other  junk food or hiding vegetables in sauces  smoothies or muffins without telling  them  all of these common feeding strategies  may seem like they work in the short run  because they get  healthy food into the bodies of our  children but they don't encourage  children to become competent intuitive  eaters as they grow up  in fact the concern is that these tricks  may actually encourage your kid to  distrust you  to distrust their own satiety signals to  distrust the forced upon  healthy foods and to obsess over certain  bad or forbidden foods basically it  causes the  opposite effect in the long run of what  we are really trying to accomplish  the reality is that hunger satiety and  body trust  are things that babies are innately  experts in  and it's usually not until they start  solids and the bribes and tricks  and little little hacks begin that they  forget how to listen to their body  so how do we give them this control back  while still  encouraging a picky eater to eat well it  starts with a division of responsibility  so what is the division of  responsibility developed by feeding  expert ellen satter  the division of responsibility is a  theory of feeding that acknowledges  children's  natural self-feeding and food regulation  capacities  and gives us as parents the  responsibility to create a supportive  environment  where our kids can thrive as competent  eaters  the division of responsibility suggests  that as parents  our job is to determine what foods are  served at meals or snacks  where that meal occurs and when that  meal occurs  our child is given the autonomy and  choice to determine  what they eat of the foods that is  served and  how much they eat or if they eat it all  now i know it might sound crazy  at first to give your toddler any sort  of freedom  when it comes to eating i mean how can  they possibly know what they need to  grow and thrive  well let me tell you in this situation  they do  and there's actual evidence to support  it the division responsibility is  also recommended as the gold standard of  feeding by a ton  of major pediatric societies and health  authorities so  this is no joke the goal of the division  responsibility is to raise a  competent eater which is a related term  to the more popular intuitive eater  now when given the freedom to eat in  response to their own hunger and  fullness cues  children are better able to eat the  amount of food that they need  and grow in a way that is right for them  in other words  they can maintain their body's settling  point because there is not that external  pressure  dictating their consumption when they're  not being pressured  they're more likely to be open to trying  new foods  and when foods are not dichotomized as  good food and bad food or  what you must eat in order to get what  you really want  they all occupy a level playing field  the result is that kids don't obsess  over sweets  or fear the healthy foods that are being  forced upon them  and that's a really good thing so now  let's break down the facts  on how you can get your picky toddler to  eat with some easy steps and tips  first model eating competence i don't  think it's reasonable to expect a child  to eat something  if their role model is not eating it as  well  and if we go even further back to when  children are starting solids  i also don't think it's reasonable to  expect a child can even  learn how to eat solids without a model  sitting with them doing the same for  older children  research also suggests that eating  together as a family is linked to better  academic performance  higher self-esteem and resilience and  lower risk of depression  substance abuse teen pregnancy eating  disorders and obesity  for these reasons i strongly suggest  always eating as a family at the dinner  table  start with just two minutes for those  really really little ones  and work your way up to 15 to 20 minutes  at the table what if your kid eats at 5  p.m and you and your husband prefer to  eat at seven  move the family schedule around to make  meals a priority  i cannot stress how important this one  step is so  if you get nothing else out of these  recommendations  make it be this if you absolutely can't  make schedules work between you  and your partner and the kids then i  suggest kind of switching things off  with your partner  or another caregiver to ensure that at  least one  person can eat with the children at  almost every meal  there will always be instances where you  you know you got to go to an event or  out for dinner and you have to let the  kids eat alone and in those situations i  suggest  at least having a snack with the child  so kind of like a mini meal of all the  same components of their meal  and then you can finish your meal out  with your partner or wherever you go  second eat family style  children like autonomy and serving a  meal family style is a great way to  reduce waste  and also give your kiddo the opportunity  to choose  what they want out of what you have  offered if you pre-plate your child's  food you risk  a overwhelming them with too much on  their plate which  often can result in them not eating at  all b  putting unsafe or fear foods on their  plate which  may upset them and freak them out of  eating anything at all  and c increase the likelihood of  waste under the recommendations of the  division of responsibility  it is imperative that we give our  children that control  third be respectful but don't cater  remember  you decide what you're going to make for  dinner and when it's going to be served  and where it's going to be served not  your toddler you do not need to  ever cater to a picky eater but the  suggestion  is to build a balanced meal that you  enjoy making and eating as a family  and to ensure that there is always one  safe food or  fall back food on the table so a safe  food is something that  assuming your child is hungry that they  will reliably  fill their belly with even if they're  not up for trying something new  or eating a food that they're just kind  of like learning to like  so serving a safe food helps our kids  trust  that they can help satisfy their own  hunger in a way that makes them feel  safe for a lot of kids let's be real  this is usually the carb food right so  bread rice pasta fruit maybe it's milk  but you know your kid best make sure  that there's a lot of this food to go  around  in case it's all your picky eater eats  fourth offer variety  satur would suggest always making sure  that there is some kind of carb  protein vegetable and fat i would agree  i mean  we want to offer a variety of the  different food groups  and let our kids choose from those  offerings as for the meals themselves  you can of course have your family  staples and favorites but i do recommend  switching things up  and offering a variety of different  carbs proteins  veggies and fruit so that your kid gets  exposure to a variety of foods  and you don't get stuck in a brutal food  rat  number five make food taste good  this is so important in this day and age  because it feels like a lot of mommy  bloggers and wellness influencers are  constantly pushing a no salt no sugar  no fat no fun lifestyle and extending it  onto  their kids poor kids right  do not be afraid to make your kids food  and your food  taste good and that often means adding  fat  some salt and some sugar these are just  tools in the chef's toolbox  so you do not need to feel guilty about  smart use of  any or all of them well of course i  allow my kid  fun foods that are packed with sugar and  salt and are easy to like regardless  i do try to focus my use on these  add-ins  to what i call more challenging foods so  for example  i want my kid to learn to like oatmeal  so i'm going to put a little bit of  maple syrup or honey on top  i want my kid to learn to like salmon so  i'm gonna add like a soy  brown sugar glaze i might want my kid to  like broccoli  so i'll serve it with a cheese sauce on  top  kids crackers and like sweet berries and  things like that are  easier for my kid to enjoy as is so  there's no need for me to  doctor them up with more sugar or a  fatty dip if i don't want to  use salt sugar and fat smartly and your  kids will be  much more likely to eat and enjoy these  naturally nourishing foods  number six stop pressuring remember the  division of responsibility rules  you did your part by choosing the foods  that would be served they now have to  decide  what they're going to eat and how much  of it they're going to eat  if they just eat their safe food and try  nothing else at that meal  you need to just kind of zip your mouth  for now and don't try to get in there to  make that meal  you know more healthy or more balanced  if something is on the table that they  don't yet  like or are learning to like you can  simply say  you don't have to eat it no pressure no  games  and over time they will let their  defense down and be more willing to try  something new  if the meal time experience is pleasant  and the more times they see it  the more likely they are to touch it the  more they touch it the more likely they  are to put it on their plate  the more they put onto their plate the  more likely they are to put it into  their mouth  and of course the more often they put in  their mouth the more likely they are to  eat it  and enjoy it this can be a long game i  get that it's frustrating as it can  typically take between  8 to 15 exposures of a food for a child  to accept it but  it is absolutely worth the wait  if we pressure we ruin our chances  potentially for life so keep the  learning experience  positive and pressure free number seven  do not react this is a really tricky one  but it's  really really important that we stop  making a big deal  of meal time when your kid does or  doesn't eat something that we want them  to eat  or not eat if we gasp and cheer the  moment that our kid puts  a carrot in their mouth they will start  to get suspicious that a carrot  is something unusual for them to like  they might not do it again  if we make a big fuss over them eating  too much of the cookies on the table  they might recognize that cookies are a  big deal  and they better eat as many as they  possibly can in case mom doesn't serve  them again  keep your cool if there's no pressure  we also have to ensure that there is no  praise if you  absolutely must say something when they  eat something that you're proud of which  i get  try something like i love this family  time or  ask them a neutral question about the  food like  what does the tofu taste like to you is  it crispy is it soft  just don't make a scene  number eight build a schedule and  reliable routine  and stick to it if you have a kid who  refuses lunch and then 20 minutes later  they're begging you for a snack  then they get to the dinner table and  push their food away  you likely need a more reliable meal and  snack schedule  if we want our kids to try something new  or eat something  other than their safe food they need to  get to that meal or snack  with an appetite i recommend ensuring  that there are at least a few hours  between each meal and snack  if your kid is whining right before a  meal because they're hungry  you can ask them if they're able to  maybe wait 15 or 20 minutes until  dinner's ready  if they can't then now is a good time to  sit them down for a little appetizer  something that is kind of part of the  meal but already ready so  cut up veggies and dip is always a  really easy option  and that brings me to number nine thou  shall not lie  or hide vegetables there are so many  books and blogs and suggestions on mommy  groups about how to trick your kids  into eating their vegetables by like  hiding them in sauces or muffins or  smoothies etc  i am not really a fan of this technique  well i am all for making food more  nutritious so of course  that means i add vegetables or nut  butters or whole grains to my recipes  whenever i possibly can  if a child is old enough to ask what is  in something  it is very important that you are  transparent and you tell them  children are incredibly smart when it  comes to picking out food that they  don't like  and by lying to them about the fact that  there's spinach in their smoothie  it communicates to them that spinach  is something that is so bad it needs to  be hidden  it needs to be lied about it also makes  our children distrust  us when it comes to food and the  division of responsibility is  all about fostering trust if a young  child  asks tell them what is in their food and  why  you can say that spinach gives it a  lovely green color or the yogurt makes  it nice and creamy and thick  you don't have to get into the fact that  it adds iron or vitamin c or whatever i  mean  communicate in language that your kids  will understand  but communicate honestly number 10  get them involved kids love control and  a lot of picky eating is about control  especially in those early toddler years  when kids are given some autonomy to  help out in some way  they're way more likely to want to try  so again  we don't want to become short order  cooks here but we could  theoretically give a child a choice  between two vegetables for dinner  do you want the broccoli or the carrots  which one sounds good to you tonight  we could also take them grocery shopping  and allow them to choose  one new fruit to try that week also  there's a lot of tasks that you can get  them involved in  when it comes to meal prep in the  kitchen so things like washing produce  adding ingredients stirring things  together etc  you are still creating the boundaries  here but you're giving them a little bit  of autonomy  to safely work within them number 11  say goodbye to dichotomous language and  get descriptive  we don't really have any food rules in  my house but  we don't use the word t-r-e-a-t  why why what is so bad about that well  just like words like junk food versus  healthy food  children are really quick to associate  foods like treat  and junk with things that they're not  supposed to have and that are  limited but highly valuable meanwhile  they see foods that are called  healthy as the ones that mom and dad  really really want them to eat  if my son asks for a treat i just don't  repeat the word back i simply call it  whatever it is so  it's cake it's a cookie it's ice cream  etc  yes of course foods are not all created  nutritionally equal but we can make them  morally equal  by neutralizing our language a lot of  parents also find themselves describing  a sweet food or junk food  as yucky to try to discourage their kid  from eating it  and yummy when they're trying to push  like a green bean on a kid  kids quickly figure out this little game  and again  they learn to distrust us so instead  describe  foods in neutral but descriptive  language  talk about broccoli looking like a tree  an asparagus  being snappy or a strawberry being nice  and juicy  talk about the cupcake as colorful and  ice cream and creamy and cold  and the cookie as chewy just don't call  it  good or bad number 12 do  sit down snacks so snacks have become  basically synonymous with treats or easy  foods in toddler speak  but really we need to start thinking of  snacks as  mini meals that offer as much balance  and nutrition  as a whole meal i recommend sitting down  as a snack  just like you would a meal and including  at least two or three different foods  but in a smaller serving so ideally  you're looking to get  two or more of what i call hunger  crushing foods so sources of fiber rich  carbs  healthy fats and protein this may also  be your time to offer a food that maybe  your child hasn't eaten a whole lot of  that day  so if your kid ate a lot of fruit at  breakfast maybe offer some crackers and  cheese for snack  if they had a lot of meat at lunch have  veggies or fruit at snack  whatever you choose just do it sitting  down number 13 is make bedtime snacks  filling not thrilling there will be days  when your kiddo just decides that they  do not want to eat anything at meal time  if this happens at breakfast or lunch  and then they ask for a snack  immediately after they get down from the  table you can remind them that you just  had a meal  but snack will be happening soon if this  happens at dinner time  i know a lot of us parents will be  worried about sending  our kid to bed with no food at all i  know i was  terrified the first time i did it  because this mama did not want a 2 am  wake up but in this case you can do a  bedtime snack  but as sadder says make it filling not  thrilling  so rather than an exciting fun food that  your kid might learn to kind of  hold out for every single night like  cookies or a popsicle  you can do a mini version of the meal  that they missed including of course a  lot of their safe food  or something really simple but not super  exciting  so milk or peanut butter on bread or  pita bread and guacamole  or yogurt parfait veggies and dip etc  filling not thrilling number 14  serve dessert with the meal  all right now let's talk about something  that you're probably really wondering  about and that's  sweets how do you get your child to stop  obsessing over dessert well there's two  ways to do this  the first is to put dessert on the same  playing field as the rest of the meal  and that means serving it with their  meal  but unlike the rest of the family style  meal which kids should  have access to on demand in whatever  amounts that they're hungry for  the dessert is a one portion only that's  the rule  they can eat it first they can eat it  last they can eat it sometime in the  middle of their meal  but once their dessert portion is gone  they have to move  on to other options on the table one is  that it doesn't teach our kids to eat  until they've kind of filled up on their  meal until they're full only to be  served dessert and then eat past  fullness in sweets  a competent intuitive eater would  ideally eat their meal until satiety and  be too full for dessert  and i've seen this happen with my son so  i do know that it is possible  the other is that we don't want them to  be putting dessert up on a pedestal  and that they must kind of drag  themselves through all the yucky food  before they get the gold this gives  dessert  far too much power now this approach  does not work for all children at all  stages in life  i found it worked great with my son up  until maybe 18 months  and then the toddler years brought on  the tantrums  some parents in this stage between like  two or three  find that they can't really explain to  their toddler why there's only  one portion so they eat dessert and then  they basically cry for the rest of the  meal time  let's be honest it is pretty hard for  anyone to feel hunger or fullness cues  when you're hysterically crying so if  you're struggling with  meal time tantrums maybe try picking  this technique up  later on when your child is old enough  to understand the one portion rule  and that they can have unlimited amounts  of everything else  number 15 offer unlimited sweets as a  snack  this is the alternative method for  dealing with sweets and the one that  i've been trying to use  now that i'm in the throes the terrible  twos so once a week or so  offer dessert at a snack in an unlimited  amount  this could mean like putting out a plate  of cookies or letting your kids eat as  much of their halloween candy as they  want  side note i did a whole other video on  halloween candy and sugar  right here but the key here is that this  unlimited sweet sesh  is far enough away from a meal that it  doesn't compete with  nutritious foods but it gives the child  the message that these foods are really  no big deal  and there's no need to hoard or obsess  over them when they're clearly  allowed to eat as much as they want a  child that is allowed  fun foods throughout the week will be  less likely to obsess over sweets  and eat a reasonable amount when they're  available this  is a really important skill that we hope  that our kids can carry on throughout  life  number 16 balance fun foods with  nutrition  if you're giving dessert or a snack that  is maybe less nutritious more often  you can balance out that fun food with  something more nourishing  so for example serving milk with cookies  or yogurt  with pie or cheese with crackers etc  this will encourage your child to put  the fun food and the nutritious food on  the same  level playing field and also will give  you the satisfaction of ensuring that  there is at least some nutrition going  in throughout the day  and finally trust your child to grow  into their healthiest happiest weight  now this is a big topic admittedly so  i'm just going to keep it  top level but it's very important to  trust this process as it pertains to  your child  and their weight don't be freaked out if  your child eats a lot less or a lot more  than other children in his cohort  children do not need to be put  on diets restricted or be force-fed  if you're not employing pressure and  bribes and tricks  a healthy child will eat the right  amount of food for their body  you need to just trust that often a very  small  child will make a parent nervous so  parents start interfering with eating  which  really only perpetuates their  disinterest in food  and often a very large child will make  parents very nervous so they restrict  and the child eats as much as they  possibly can when they get the chance  if you're not doing these things and  your child is still not eating or  growing well  then it's really important to speak to  your pediatrician about other possible  feeding issues  including oral motor development tongue  ties  behavioral concerns and developmental  delays okay so now i want to cover some  commonly asked questions about  dealing with picky eaters one what  happens if he only eats  the safe food if you've been pressuring  your kid to eat healthy foods or  restricting fun foods in the past  it's very likely that your kid is going  to only eat their safe food  or only eat dessert first it's natural  for a child to see the forbidden food  be served and try to get as much as they  possibly can  before you go back to your old  pressuring ways give it  time your child will need to learn how  to trust  that you're not going to cut them off  and you're not going to force them to  try something that they're not yet  comfortable with  remember keep your cool keep putting the  vegetables or whatever  food it is that they're learning to like  on the table  but not expecting or insisting that they  eat it  keep being neutral when they pick it up  put it on their plate or put it in their  mouth  keep trying to make the food taste good  with a little bit of salt or sugar or  fat  whatever and of course expose  expose expose now next question  what if i run out of the safe food so  ideally  we want to try to ensure that there's  enough of that safe food to go around  but of course our fridges and pantries  are not magically replenishing  so i often sometimes run out of fruit  because my family eats so much of it  so if a safe food is in limited quantity  you may want to offer two safe foods on  the table so  this for example could be maybe fruit  and cheese  what if he eats nothing at all do not  panic  it happens see my tips on offering a  filling  not thrilling bedtime snack and for  younger toddlers this might be the time  for their milk  keep in mind that hunger is actually  naturally suppressed by melatonin so  in my experience even the nights that my  kid eats nothing at all  he drinks his milk sleeps through the  night wakes up at a normal time  and just has a bigger breakfast you will  be surprised how amazingly resilient  they are  what if dessert is his safe food and he  just wants more of it  if you choose to serve dessert with the  meal keep in mind that this should not  be the safe food  since we really need to stick to that  one dessert serving rule so that way  it doesn't crowd out other nutritious  foods shouldn't we teach our kids that  sugar is  bad for them children are born with a  neutral attitude towards food  if we start saying things like sugar is  poison or candy is bad for you  kids will start thinking that all foods  that are bad  actually taste really good and they  develop a skewed relationship with food  keep language neutral and just call a  food what it is  it's not a treat it's not bad it's a  cookie it's a lollipop it's a cake  next how much should my child eat  so a lot of people will give you these  specifics about how many tablespoons of  food per month or year  of life a child should eat but how much  your child  needs is so highly variable between  children  and within children between days that i  find that these guidelines  just stress parents out more and force  them into restriction  or pressuring behaviors i prefer to  serve food family styles so that kids  can therefore choose  what they want to put on their plate and  you can limit the waste  if you're pre-portioning your child's  meal give them just  one or two pieces of each thing and let  them  ask or motion for more what if my child  is overweight or  underweight according to the division of  responsibility it doesn't matter what  the current size of your child is  the recommendations here are the same do  not pressure the child to eat or  restrict foods  and their weight will fall where it's  supposed to and yes  even overweight children should have  access to fun foods  and desserts without it being used as a  bribe  restriction and pressure often will do  the opposite  of what you intend and will only make a  small child eat less  and a large child eat more than what  their body  actually needs now what about the no  thank you bite or  one polite bite i get why parents use  this but  it's actually against the tenets of the  division of responsibility  it teaches children that they are not  allowed to trust their body  when it says no because they have to do  something for an adult  we want to teach our kids to be able to  trust their inner gauge  when it tells them that something isn't  right  and this extends far beyond food to  other important areas of life like  strangers for example so instead of the  one bite rule  you can teach your kids that you respect  them to listen  and trust their body you can teach them  if they do not want to eat something  to say no thank you i'm still learning  to like  beans for example if they don't like  anything that i've offered should i make  them a separate meal  if you have offered a safe food and they  still are not interested  in eating consider that they may  genuinely just  not be very hungry and you can try again  at snack time  you really don't want to cater to each  child by making separate meals when they  don't eat  or don't like what's being originally  served or they may just learn  that they can hold out on dinner to get  the mac and cheese or the pizza or the  chicken nuggets that they really like  stay strong and just trust that if you  did your part by offering the safe food  that you need to just let them do theirs  what if my kid sees a favorite food on  the table  while they're having lunch and starts  screaming that they just want the banana  so i put this question in there because  this is what my kid sometimes does  all the time so i figure i'm not alone  remember  you are the adult you decide what foods  are being served  and if you didn't choose the banana as  the safe food or  as an option at that particular meal you  don't have to give in  i don't you can simply say we can have  the banana for a snack  this afternoon or tomorrow but right now  we're having cherries  and you've still got lots of different  foods on the table to choose from  whatever it is that they're crying about  just make sure that you do put it into  the rotation often enough  that it doesn't become a novelty so for  example  my son knows that there are rainbow  sprinkles in the cupboard  and sometimes he will ask for sprinkles  on his food i know  he likes sprinkles so a few times a week  i will preemptively  kind of add them to his meal you want to  be preemptive about these things  that you don't get into the habit of  them having to throw a tantrum to get  foods that they like  then you give in and then they learn  that screaming at the table is how you  get what you really want  are there any non-pressure tips to get  my toddler to try or eat new foods  honestly there are a lot of fun ways to  encourage kids to try new foods  without having to resort to food rules  and pressure  some examples that i love are using new  fun utensils cutting food  into fun shapes with cookie cutters  serving food in a fun bento box or a  colorful container that they like  talking about the food on the table  without pressure like show me which food  is green  show me which food is blue and honestly  just keep putting the food on the table  and modeling good eating behavior i want  to remind you that none of this is meant  to shame a parent for how they feed  their child  if what you are doing is working for you  amazing  keep it up moms and dads but i want to  share this evidence-based approach with  you for raising a healthy  competent intuitive eater since so many  of you struggling with picky eating have  asked and this really is the gold  standard on how to approach  feeding if you want to print this out i  have a picky eaters  cheat sheet that you can download when  you subscribe to my blog so  i'm going to leave a link to that below  and on that note if you liked this video  be sure to give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below if you have any  questions as it pertains to  intuitive eating or child nutrition or  child feeding picky eating etc  subscribe to the channel and i will see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye  you
 

